WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.876
Preflight resilience begins with checking navigation status, home point,

00:00:03.876 --> 00:00:07.233
compass or heading consistency, maps, warnings, firmware state,

00:00:07.233 --> 00:00:10.589
and expected flight modes according to approved procedures.

00:00:10.589 --> 00:00:14.785
An unexplained anomaly before launch is a reason to stop.

00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:18.561
Plan a simple route that can be understood visually.

00:00:18.561 --> 00:00:23.596
Maintain visual line of sight as required and appropriate, identify stable landmarks,

00:00:23.596 --> 00:00:30.309
and avoid placing the aircraft where loss of automated position would immediately threaten people or obstacles.

00:00:30.309 --> 00:00:35.764
Manual-control readiness means training for the aircraft's degraded behavior in authorized safe conditions,

00:00:35.764 --> 00:00:40.798
understanding mode changes, and knowing the controls without improvisation during an incident.

00:00:40.798 --> 00:00:43.735
It does not mean experimenting with interference.

00:00:43.735 --> 00:00:44.994
Use conservative automation.

00:00:44.994 --> 00:00:50.029
Verify each waypoint, route boundary, home point, return setting, and emergency area.

00:00:50.029 --> 00:00:54.644
Define abort criteria for navigation warnings, cue disagreement, drifting, mode changes,

00:00:54.644 --> 00:00:57.162
unexpected route behavior, or low confidence.

00:00:57.162 --> 00:01:02.196
Identify landing areas along the mission, not only at the launch point.

00:01:02.196 --> 00:01:05.553
Consider wind, terrain, people, property, access, and battery.

00:01:05.553 --> 00:01:11.847
A nearby controlled landing may be safer than a long automated return through uncertain navigation.

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:16.881
Document the plan, warnings, receiver status, visual references, settings, and crew roles.

00:01:16.881 --> 00:01:21.077
Resilience comes from independent cues, practiced decisions, and preserved margin,

00:01:21.077 --> 00:01:25.273
not from assuming that more automation will overcome unreliable inputs.

00:01:25.273 --> 00:01:29.469
Rehearse decisions through checklists and tabletop discussion using hypothetical warnings.

00:01:29.469 --> 00:01:32.825
Crews can practice communication, automation disengagement, landing selection,

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:37.860
and reporting without transmitting interference or creating risk to real navigation systems.
